+# SDK transaction building and auto-derivation: performance design
+
+**Status:** exploration and design. No production code is changed by this document.
+**Worktree:** `/Code/cashscript/.worktrees/sdk-tx-perf`, branch `perf/sdk-tx-perf`, base `fcbd58a09`.
+**Date:** 2026-10-22.
+**Revision:** 2026-10-22, fourth pass after review. It adds per-evaluation suppression, the final-repair
+clean exit, the derivation-scoped provisional mark, and the production-path measurement.
+**Scope:** `packages/solidcash` transaction building, stateful derivation, and the framed composer.
+**Superseded in part:** [`SPEC.md`](SPEC.md) is the implementation spec. It replaces the step plan and
+acceptance criteria that used to live in sections 11-12; the history, prior art, measurements and
+design rationale here remain the reference.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+The SDK builds a transaction by **learning one value per VM probe**. A probe assembles the full
+transaction, evaluates one input, and reads the first failed `OP_EQUALVERIFY`: the declaration and the
+value the body computed. The SDK applies that one value, rebuilds, and probes again. The shape is a
+fixed-point iteration whose cost is linear in the number of independently verified outputs.
+
+The main proposal is to **reinstate the relaxed discovery probe** that this repository shipped between
+2026-08-21 and 2026-09-13, rescoped as a collector: suppress a failed comparison only when the
+comparison is authenticated, record every such comparison in one run, apply the whole layer, and treat a
+run that suppresses nothing as the faithful verdict. The earlier implementation is prior art, including
+its exclusions and its known failure modes; §2 records them.
+
+The measured prototype converts `N + 1` probes into one discovery run on a controlled contract, cutting
+the derivation segment by 3.6-8.6x and the whole call by 1.9-3.5x. Those figures are an **upper bound**:
+the prototype suppressed every failed verify, it ran in the same process as the checks that were meant to
+catch mistakes, and no clean run happened. The corrected estimate for one discovery plus one clean run is
+1.3-1.7x at N=4 and 1.8-3.8x at N=8-16 on derivation, or roughly 5-65 ms per call.
+
+The first production lever is separate: at standard consensus limits the send path eagerly runs
+`covenantBuilder.debug()` before rebuilding. On the measured stateful file that stage cost 4.82 s over 20
+sends against 33 ms on the relaxed profile, while phase-2 derivation stayed at 243 ms. Gating that probe
+comes first; the recorder swap, the collector, and the framed-composer batching follow in dependency
+order.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Prior art: relaxed discovery already existed
+
+Relaxed discovery is the same mechanism this document proposed in its first revision. It was built,
+widened, and removed:
+
+| commit | date | content |
+|---|---|---|
+| `e7d67cabf` | 2026-08-21 | "fix: generalize atomic participant execution" introduced `relaxNextVerification`, `relaxOutputVerifications`, `relaxedProbeVmCache` |
+| `bfe645326` | 2026-08-25 | "keep mixed-width integers and single-NFT probes faithful": relaxed multi-pin discovery stays off for one-NFT layouts |
+| `95f410265` | 2026-08-26 | "relax multi-entry mapping probes to pin every NFT in one discovery pass": mapping-only functions; not `stateCount == 1`, not fixed-size arrays; three-key insert 4 -> 2 probes |
+| `b2fab244a` | 2026-08-27 | "pin auto-derivation probes on the first discovery pass": optimistic ABI-state seeding plus relaxation |
+| `28e5c4660` | 2026-09-13 | "fix(sdk): authenticate kernel result and chunk resources" (parent) |
+| `8d335f51f` | 2026-09-13 | "fix(kernel): authenticate chunkable row resources": removes relaxation |
+
+### 2.1 Why it was removed
+
+The commit message is a subject line with no body, and the later checkpoint commits (`b93e6bb9f`,
+`83f14c0d1`, `608e35a20`) squash the branch history, so there is no recorded rationale. The code and
+the surrounding commits support one reading, stated here as an inference:
+
+- The removal sits in a two-commit "authenticate ... resources" series. The same commit adds exact
+  `describeValueChunkWrites` resource identities, `internalArgNames` ownership of continuation
+  witnesses, `physicalOwnerId` ownership of occupied value-chunk bases, and monotone semantic claims in
+  `message-bus/compose.ts`.
+- The removed path's own comment said the candidate "is only a graph claim: the converged transaction
+  still passes the ordinary, non-relaxed BCH VM preflight." A pin read from a suppressed verification
+  is not an authenticated observation.
+- The kernel work needed every resource claim authenticated. A suppressed pin is a candidate, so it
+  could not feed those claims.
+
+This is an inference from the diff, not a recorded reason. The reviewer has no independent record of it
+either. Treat the inference as true for design purposes: unconfirmed values must never reach the kernel's
+resource claims. §7.4 does that with a provisional mark.
+
+### 2.2 What the old implementation excluded, and why
+
+The removed code did not suppress every verification. Its own comments and the commits above record:
+
+- **Single-NFT layouts stayed faithful.** After the first failed equality, later stack garbage could be
+  read as the state commitment.
+- **Fixed-size arrays stayed faithful.** The same clobbering applied to `kind === 'array'` writes.
+- **The check after `OP_INPUTBYTECODE` was never relaxed.** A deferred keccak probe deliberately fails
+  that equality so the runtime preimage can be learned; suppressing it would change execution semantics.
+- **Runtime-loop transfers inside `OP_INVOKE` bodies used the faithful reduced trace.** The sparse
+  observer could not see introspection executed inside invoked bodies.
+
+A reinstated collector must keep these behaviours until each is measured away.
+
+### 2.3 What remains in the tree
+
+- The comment block describing relaxed discovery is still at
+  `stateful/sc-derive-full-phase2-iteration-probe.ts:143`, in the function this design would change.
+- `firstWriteWins` is **live and independent of relaxation**. It predates it (`d673e3e39`, 2026-08-05)
+  and 11 extractor call sites pass `true` at this revision. The old relaxation reused it at the two
+  phase-2 sites in `stateful/sc-derive-full-phase2-apply-state-entries.ts`, which now pass `false`.
+  A reinstated collector should keep it: it is what stops a later comparison from overwriting an
+  earlier correct pin.
+- `test/e2e/OptimisticMultiPinDerivation.test.ts` still asserts `lastDerivationIterations === 1` after
+  the removal. Those cases pass through the optimistic seeding and pinning added in `b2fab244a`; the
+  dropped piece is the suppressed-verification fallback for values the seeding cannot know.
+
+---
+
+## 3. How the SDK derives today
+
+### 3.1 The probe primitive
+
+`evaluateProbeTrace` (`stateful/faithful-probe-trace.ts:956`) runs one input of an assembled
+transaction. Three modes exist:
+
+| mode | selected when | retains |
+|---|---|---|
+| libauth `debug()` | `maximumInputBytes <= 10_000`, or a debug override | every program state (thousands) |
+| faithful shallow recorder | above ordinary capacity, `forceFullDebug` | every state, sharing the function table |
+| sparse observer | relaxed/private-network limits | projected observation states only, six opcodes, plus a 96-state full-stack window |
+
+At standard limits the code chooses `debug()` (`faithful-probe-trace.ts:978`). The parity harness at
+`:987` compares `debug()` against the **faithful recorder** while `useFaithfulRecorder` is true. It
+does not compare `debug()` against the sparse observer.
+
+### 3.2 The loops that learn one value at a time
+
+| loop | code | passes |
+|---|---|---|
+| stateful phase 2 | `StatefulContract.ts:5894`, `sc-derive-full-phase2-iteration-probe.ts` | 20 default, 64 with loop/chunk vars, or the artifact's published ceiling |
+| phase-1 progressive entries | `sc-derive-full-phase1-progressive-entries.ts:150` | `2*entrySlots + 2*writes + 1` |
+| post-loop cleaned rescan | `sc-derive-full-phase2-cleaned-rescan.ts:44` | 64 |
+| final computed-output repair | `stb-converged-final-transaction.ts:194` | `3*outputs + 1` |
+| framed declaration walk | `framed/composer/draft.ts:922` | one value per iteration, `attempts = views + witnesses` |
+| framed plain-path loop | `framed/call/call.ts:1847`, `:2539` | one attempt per learned value |
+
+The code states the mechanism in several places. `sc-derive-full-phase2-apply-loop-entries.ts:55`:
+"The VM halts at the first mismatched OP_EQUALVERIFY, so each probe reveals one more commitment."
+`framed/composer/draft.ts:923`: "Each iteration learns at most one value."
+
+### 3.3 Measured cost of the current shape
+
+`docs/framed/NOPROXY_LANE_COST.md` §7 gives whole-title counters for no-proxy `take job`:
+
+| counter, whole title | before frame-scoped settlement | after the port |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| title wall | 383.1 s | 61.1 s |
+| program evaluations | 3,047 | 1,014 |
+| stepped walks | 1,782 | 274 |
+| scoped derivations | 83 | 0 |
+
+The `takeJob` composition alone was 2,242 evaluations and 1,406 walks before the port. Do not pair the
+composition's "before" figures with the whole title's "after" figures.
+
+An earlier run in §6 of the same document counted, for the same title: 128 plain-path attempts, 127
+hosts, 60 whole-composition and 67 scoped derivations, 2,120 declaration reads (664 answered from a
+recorded close and 1,456 walked), 2,362 evaluations and 1,457 walks. **869 of those 2,120 reads were not
+closes at all.** Those reads stop `learnComputedValues` (`draft.ts:936`), so batching closes does not
+remove them. Only 190 of the 2,120 reads repeat a program (`draft.ts:931`), so a shared walk cannot
+answer them either: they hit different programs.
+
+The counters do not establish what the remaining 1,014 evaluations and 274 walks consist of. The earlier
+claim that they are "re-derivations" and "one-value reads" was an interpretation, not a measurement.
+
+### 3.4 Memory shape
+
+- A production `debug()` probe retains every state. The SDK's own comment
+  (`network/createVirtualMachine.ts:790`) cites a maximal 9,817-byte input reaching 77,216 retained
+  states at 63-68 MB and 0.6-0.9 s. The trace is reduced afterwards, so most of that is transient.
+- `copyOfPlainData` copies the whole program after an evaluation **only when the run stopped on a
+  recorded close** (`network/createVirtualMachine.ts:1213`). In a composed title that is a small
+  fraction of the runs; measure the share before optimizing it.
+- The progressive-entry loop keeps a hex-encoded copy of the complete transaction per pass
+  (`sc-derive-full-phase1-progressive-entries.ts:125/147/226`). A relay-standard transaction is at most
+  about 100 KB (400 KB of hex); the 1.3 MB mock transactions make this look larger than production.
+- `TransactionBuilder.build()` returns a hex string; internal consumers re-decode it
+  (`TransactionBuilder.ts:1367`).
+- On a standard-limit send, `runSendDiscoveryProbe` calls `covenantBuilder.debug()` eagerly
+  (`stb-run-send-discovery-probe.ts:24`). The code comment at `:51` says the rebuild depends on what the
+  probe finds. That claim was not verified in this pass, so keep a byte-identity gate before removing
+  the probe.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Measurement setup
+
+- Worktree `/Code/cashscript/.worktrees/sdk-tx-perf`, branch `perf/sdk-tx-perf`, base `fcbd58a09`.
+- `yarn install --frozen-lockfile` with Yarn 1.22.22, Node 22.22.0. The postinstall build ran
+  `tsc -b tsconfig.packages.json` and the testing-suite compile; 99 s total.
+- Symlink audit after install: 224 symlinks, all resolve inside the worktree. None resolve into
+  `/Code/cashscript`. Workspace links point at `packages/*` in this worktree; `.bin` links point at
+  `node_modules/*` in this worktree; no tracked symlinks exist.
+- Benchmarks used `MockNetworkProvider` with its default limits (`maximumInputBytes` 1,000,000,
+  `operationCostBudgetPerByte` 800), so the SDK selects the sparse observer.
+- The stateful benchmark compiled a contract inline with `compileString` and used the repository's own
+  test fixtures (`createTestProvider`, `setupStatefulUtxos`). Construction and funding were outside the
+  timed region; only `.send()` was timed.
+- The suppression prototype was an environment-gated patch in
+  `packages/solidcash/src/network/createVirtualMachine.ts` (`SOLIDC_EXPERIMENT_SUPPRESS_VERIFY=1`). It
+  cleared the error after a failed `OP_EQUALVERIFY` and let the run continue. It was removed after
+  measurement.
+
+### 4.1 Benchmark contracts
+
+```solidcash
+// entry: N computed mapping inserts, values not supplied as arguments
+contract C() {
+  mapping(int => int) bal;
+  function setAll(int[] ks, int[] vs, pubkey pk, sig sg) {
+    require(checkSig(sg, pk));
+    for (uint i = 0; i < ks.length; i = i + 1) { bal[ks[i]] = int(i) + 7; }
+  }
+}
+
+// event: the same insert plus one event per iteration
+contract C() {
+  mapping(int => int) bal;
+  event Tick(int indexed i);
+  function setAll(int[] ks, int[] vs, pubkey pk, sig sg) { ... emit Tick(int(i)); }
+}
+```
+
+Values are computed in the body rather than passed, which is the case that forces one committed value
+per entry. A values-as-arguments variant converges in one probe without any change because the planner
+computes the commitments off-chain.
+
+### 4.2 What the prototype does and does not establish
+
+The patch modified `buildVirtualMachineBch2026ForProvider`, the factory both the final preflight
+(`message-bus/preflight.ts:1127`) and the mock verifier
+(`network/createPreparedTxVerifier.ts:65`) build their VMs from. With suppression on, the first probe
+had no error, so the loop exited after one pass and **no clean run happened**. The mock verifier can
+also consume a one-use receipt from the SDK's own preflight and skip its VM pass
+(`createPreparedTxVerifier.ts:169`).
+
+The prototype therefore shows that the discovery run can **reach and record** every placeholder
+comparison on this contract. It does not independently establish that the learned values are correct,
+because the checks that would have caught a wrong value were patched as well. The values were probably
+correct: the benchmark's values do not depend on each other, and the final transaction passed the
+patched preflight. That is not a soundness proof.
+
+The prototype was also one run per cell with no repetitions. The ratios below are indicative; the
+probes counts are deterministic and are the reliable signal.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Measured prototype results
+
+Warm second repetition, one process, mock limits. `probes` is `lastDerivationIterations` (evaluated
+probes; the final fixed-point check is not counted). Milliseconds are wall time for `.send()`.
+
+| shape | probes | derive ms | call ms | probes with suppression | derive ms | call ms |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| entry, N=4 | 5 | 29 | 43.3 | 1 | 8 | 23.2 |
+| event, N=4 | 6 | 38 | 62.3 | 1 | 8 | 25.1 |
+| entry, N=8 | 9 | 60 | 95.3 | 1 | 11 | 41.9 |
+| event, N=8 | 10 | 76 | 101.1 | 1 | 11 | 33.6 |
+| entry, N=16 | 17 | 121 | 140.6 | 1 | 14 | 39.7 |
+| event, N=16 | 18 | 133 | 167.2 | 1 | 24 | 77.8 |
+
+The prototype keeps suppression enabled for every probe, so it measures the discovery pass alone. A
+production implementation adds one clean run, which is the faithful verdict.
+
+One discovery plus one clean run is two probes. Using the baseline minima in §5.1 and the prototype's
+suppressed-run costs:
+
+| shape | derivation baseline | discovery + clean (est.) | derivation speedup |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| entry, N=4 | 21 ms | ~14-16 ms | 1.3-1.5x |
+| entry, N=8 | 41 ms | ~20-22 ms | 1.8-2.0x |
+| entry, N=16 | 94 ms | ~25-29 ms | 3.2-3.8x |
+| event, N=4 | 27 ms | ~16-18 ms | 1.5-1.7x |
+| event, N=8 | 56 ms | ~22-24 ms | 2.3-2.5x |
+| event, N=16 | 111 ms | ~40-48 ms | 2.3-2.8x |
+
+The whole call includes build, signing, funding, verification and submission, so its saving is smaller:
+roughly 5-65 ms per call, or 1.2-2.4x at these sizes. The values are independent on this contract; a
+dependent value adds one layer, which is one more probe (`d + 1`), not `2d`.
+
+### 5.1 Baseline variance
+
+The kept harness (`test/e2e/SdkTxPerfProbe.test.ts`) runs one untimed warm-up per shape and size, then
+five timed repetitions with the shapes interleaved and their order alternated. Without the prototype,
+one process, mock limits, minimum of five:
+
+| shape | derivation min | whole call min |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| entry, N=4 | 21 ms | 32.3 ms |
+| entry, N=8 | 41 ms | 59.0 ms |
+| entry, N=16 | 94 ms | 114.0 ms |
+| event, N=4 | 27 ms | 40.3 ms |
+| event, N=8 | 56 ms | 77.7 ms |
+| event, N=16 | 111 ms | 131.4 ms |
+
+The spread across the five reps reached 1.7x on one cell (entry N=8, 41-70 ms). Use the minima for
+comparisons and the probe counts as the reliable signal. The prototype table above is a single run on
+the earlier harness; its suppressed-run costs are used only as per-probe approximations.
+
+### 5.2 Per-probe VM cost at production limits
+
+A synthetic 9,804-state script (`OP_1 OP_DROP` pairs, 9,809-byte locking bytecode) on the installed
+libauth fork:
+
+| call | states retained | ms | retained heap |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| `vm.debug(program)` | 9,804 | 72.5 | ~5 MB |
+| `vm.evaluate(program)` | 1 | 4.5 | ~0 MB |
+
+The script has no `OP_DEFINE` function table, so it understates what `debug()` costs on a real
+outlined contract. The installed fork already shares the function table in
+`partiallyCloneProgramState`, so the difference against the faithful recorder must be measured on a
+real contract rather than inferred from stock libauth.
+
+### 5.3 Where the extra production time goes
+
+Two e2e files, run with `SOLIDC_DEBUG_BUILDER_TIMING=1`.
+
+Relaxed (mock defaults, sparse observer, discovery probe skipped):
+
+| file | tests | test time | derive passes | derive time | share of test time |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| `StatefulContract.test.ts` | 73 | 3.18 s | 18 | 124 ms | 3.9% |
+| `DynamicLoopEntry.test.ts` | 4 | 0.81 s | 11 | 125 ms | 15.4% |
+
+Standard probe mode (mock with `maximumInputBytes: 10_000`; the mock still declares `simulator = true`,
+so `usesFastVmPaths` keeps the fast VM paths; this is **not** a live network):
+
+| file | test time | derive passes | derive time | share of test time |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| `StatefulContract.test.ts` | 7.93 s | 18 | 243 ms | 3.1% |
+| `DynamicLoopEntry.test.ts` | 2.36 s | 11 | 302 ms | 12.8% |
+
+The 10k profile added about 4.75 s of test time, but the phase-2 derive passes grew by only 0.12 s. The
+send-timing stages show where the rest went (sums over the 20 sends in `StatefulContract.test.ts`):
+
+| stage | relaxed | 10k |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| `post-discovery-build` | 33 ms | 4,824 ms |
+| `initial-build` | 36 ms | 43 ms |
+| `covenants-converged` | 41 ms | 42 ms |
+| `provider-accepted` | 36 ms | 42 ms |
+| `final-build` | 28 ms | 32 ms |
+| `final-materialization` | 24 ms | 23 ms |
+
+`post-discovery-build` is the stage after the eager `covenantBuilder.debug()` and the rebuild that follows
+it (`stb-run-send-discovery-probe.ts:22` and `:56`). Its extra 4.79 s accounts for the test-time increase;
+phase-2 derivation is 243 ms in the same run. Phase-1 probes are not separately counted; the stages that
+contain them (`initial-build`, `covenants-converged`) did not move.
+
+This is a **lower bound** for a live network. The patched mock still declares `simulator = true`, so
+`usesFastVmPaths` keeps the fast paths (`createVirtualMachine.ts:118`).
+
+A live-like run (`simulator = false`, standard limits) failed eight deploy tests, all with
+`cannot prove coin …: the provider served no transaction`. The cause is `ownCoinRefusal`
+(`framed/deploy/own-coin.ts:65`): the mock's synthesized coins need `simulator === true` and
+`getLedgerOutput`; with the flag off, the check falls through to `getTxDetails`, where those coins have no
+transaction. That is a harness constraint, not evidence that deploy depends on the fast P2SH evaluator.
+No VM-path failure was observed; all eight were coin proof. A live-like harness must present
+`simulator = false` only to the VM factory, or use real transactions. The patch was reverted; the
+discovery stage can only be larger on a live network.
+
+**Conclusions:**
+
+- The eager discovery debug is the first production lever. The recorder swap (step 2) does not touch it,
+  because the discovery probe goes through `TransactionBuilder.debug()` and `debugLibauthTemplate`, not
+  `evaluateProbeTrace`.
+- These two files are the **wrong workload** for the collector. The target is a loop-heavy call: at 10k,
+  `DynamicLoopEntry.test.ts` spent 302 ms over 11 derive passes, about 27 ms per probe, and that is a
+  lower bound because the simulator keeps the fast VM paths. An N=16 loop pays 17 of those probes; the
+  collector pays 2. The same per-probe cost applies to the phase-2 loop on a live network, where every
+  probe is a full `debug()` run.
+- The framed composer needs the same mechanism in its own loop. The 61 s ladder titles are
+  framed-composer time: `take job` does 1,014 evaluations and 274 walks per title over a composed
+  transaction of about 670 KB, and the last profile was VM-bound. The collector cannot skip a walk that
+  learns nothing; the structural recipe cache in step 4 is what removes those.
+- The correctness risk is bounded: the final preflight stays authoritative and byte identity gates each
+  step. What is at stake is development time, not users' funds. The collector is not deferred.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Why one run can learn a layer
+
+The covenant is a conjunction of equalities over outputs. Filling every placeholder correctly makes
+every comparison pass. If a comparison fails only because the output still holds a placeholder, the
+failure carries no information about the rest of the program: the operands are popped either way, and
+`OP_EQUALVERIFY` does not branch. Suppression is stack-equivalent **for that comparison**.
+
+It is not globally equivalent. A later comparison can compute its value from an output that still holds
+a different placeholder, so its computed operand is wrong. That is why the result is a **candidate
+layer**, not an accepted run, and why the loop needs a run that suppresses nothing before any build is
+finalized. It is also the source of the
+historical stack-garbage failures: a later equality can compare the wrong operand and overwrite an
+earlier correct pin unless the extractor keeps the first write.
+
+The learned values form layers:
+
+- **Layer 0:** values computed from arguments, constants, and the pre-state. One run learns them.
+- **Layer 1:** values computed from a layer-0 output (a callee return, a chunk length, a linked sorted
+  node). A second run learns them.
+- **Layer n+1:** values that read a layer-n value.
+
+The number of passes becomes the dependency depth instead of the number of outputs. The prototype
+measured depth 1 for both contracts at every N.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Design: reinstate relaxed discovery as a collector
+
+### 7.1 Mechanism
+
+Suppress **only `OP_EQUALVERIFY`**, and in step 3 only when the pending introspection is
+`OP_OUTPUTTOKENCOMMITMENT`. It is the only comparison in `PROBE_TRACE_OPCODES` whose operands the
+commitment extractor consumes. Suppressing `OP_NUMEQUALVERIFY` or `OP_EQUAL` followed by `OP_VERIFY`
+would let a run skip past checks nothing learns from; the removed code also handled only
+`OP_EQUALVERIFY`.
+
+Value pins are handled differently in the implementation spec. `SPEC.md` C3 and `spec/collector-probe.md`
+extend the shared pairing classes to `OP_OUTPUTVALUE`, `OP_OUTPUTBYTECODE` and `OP_OUTPUTTOKENAMOUNT`,
+with suppression gated on `ownsValueOutput` (never on `OP_OUTPUTTOKENCATEGORY`). This paragraph records why
+the earlier plan kept them faithful: `extractVerifiedValuesFromTrace`
+(`trace-verified-value-helpers.ts:214`) has its own 16-step pairing, `expectedOperandOnTop` flag and
+`observedActual` check, so the commitment helper alone cannot be shown no looser. The SPEC's second
+pairing class is that helper; it lands with the collector, not in a later step.
+
+The hook exists only on the observer path. At standard limits `evaluateProbeTrace` calls
+`cachedDebugVm()` (`faithful-probe-trace.ts:118`), which is built without an observer, so there is no
+probe-only operation wrapper to attach to. The removed code quietly sent relaxed probes to the sparse
+observer at standard limits, which is the path whose production fidelity is unproven.
+
+The plan takes the discovery-debug lever first (§5.3, §8.1), then does the faithful-recorder swap (§8.2), then
+opts suppression in per evaluation at the collector's call sites: the recorder at standard limits and the
+observer at relaxed limits. A relaxed-only collector is
+not a separate step; §5.3 shows its share of a real workload is small and it leaves live networks on the
+old loop.
+
+Do not install suppression in `buildVirtualMachineBch2026ForProvider` unconditionally: the final
+preflight and the mock verifier build their VMs there.
+
+Suppression is opted into **per evaluation**, never attached to a shared cached VM. The observer, recorder
+and debug VMs are cached per provider (`faithful-probe-trace.ts:65-79`) and shared by callers that must
+stay faithful: phase-1 return/event probes, runtime-effect traces, the framed proxy deploy, route
+settlement, and the `forceFullDebug` path that keeps runtime-loop transfers faithful. Attaching
+suppression to those VMs would silently change all of them. Use a separate relaxed VM cache, as the
+removed implementation did, or a per-call flag set only by the collector's call sites.
+
+1. Read the two operands before the comparison.
+2. Run the original operation.
+3. If it failed and the comparison is **authenticated** (§7.3), record
+   `(declared, computed, instruction identity, ip)` and return the state with `error` cleared.
+4. Continue. Every later authenticated placeholder comparison is reached and recorded.
+
+Two rules stay permanent:
+
+- the equality following `OP_INPUTBYTECODE` is never suppressed; deferred keccak learning depends on it
+  failing;
+- the final preflight always runs without the probe wrapper.
+
+The single-NFT (`stateCount == 1`) and fixed-size-array (`kind === 'array'`) exclusions are **provisional
+carve-outs**, not permanent: step 3 tries to lift them with tests. Runtime-loop transfers inside
+`OP_INVOKE` bodies use the faithful reduced trace until converged.
+
+Bound the recording (for example 4,096 comparisons per run) and stop suppressing at the bound.
+
+Suppression is decided inside the VM, before any extractor sees the trace. The wrapper must keep its own
+pending-introspection record and the output index that introspection named, and its rule must be **no
+looser than the extractor's**: the same push/`OP_INVOKE` tolerance and the same value check. A predicate
+looser than the extractor's would suppress a failure the extractor never records, and the run would skip
+the check without learning anything. Share one pairing helper with `extractCommitmentMapFromTrace`; if the
+wrapper cannot decide, do not suppress. The removed implementation used `relaxNextVerification` for this
+role.
+
+### 7.2 Pairing
+
+Do **not** require the introspection at `ip - 1`. The compiler outlines the introspection and its
+equality into different invocation frames, and the existing extractor already handles the real shapes
+(`stateful/trace-commitment-helpers.ts`):
+
+- pushes and `OP_INVOKE` may sit between `OP_OUTPUTTOKENCOMMITMENT` and the equality (`:124`);
+- both `<expected> OTC EQUALVERIFY` and `OTC <expected> EQUALVERIFY` occur, notably for return values
+  (`:149`);
+- an invoked helper can perform its own equality while an outer observation is pending; the extractor
+  keeps a stack and only consumes a pair whose operand names the transaction's value (`:160`).
+
+The collector should emit the same projected trace shape the extractor already consumes, and let the
+extractor pair. `reduceProbeTrace`'s comment (`faithful-probe-trace.ts:693`) states the same rule: the
+introspection and its equality may be in different frames.
+
+### 7.3 Authentication
+
+A comparison is authenticated when **both** hold:
+
+1. One operand equals the transaction's current value at that output. The extractor already checks this
+   (`actualCommitment !== below && actualCommitment !== top`); it is what prevents an invoked helper's
+   unrelated equality from consuming an outer observation.
+2. The output is one the SDK seeded, planned, or learned. The STB knows its placeholders, entries,
+   events, chunks and transfers by coordinate.
+
+Do not authenticate by `ip - 1` adjacency. Do not authenticate only by "`declared` equals a
+placeholder": after the first apply, those outputs no longer hold placeholders, so layer 1 and later
+would have nothing to authenticate against. Value-plus-ownership covers every layer.
+
+### 7.4 Convergence: one probe per layer, with a provisional mark
+
+A discovery run that suppressed nothing is the faithful verdict. A `d`-layer derivation costs `d + 1`
+probes. The derivation loop **never exits while the mark is set**: on any exit (fixed point, iteration
+cap, stable key, no progress), turn suppression off, run one clean probe, and let its result decide. That
+covers `.build()`, `.send()` and composed sends at once. The per-path refusals are assertions, not the
+primary guarantee.
+
+No tentative overlay is needed. Keep writing builder state as today; mark the derivation **provisional**
+while any currently applied value came from a suppressing run, clear the mark on a run that suppressed
+nothing. Derivation writes through roughly 30 builder methods, and many change output geometry; a
+coordinate-keyed overlay goes stale as soon as a candidate adds an output. The mark gives the same
+guarantee without tracking coordinates or restoring state.
+
+```
+provisional = false
+suppression = true
+seen = new Set()
+
+loop:
+  result = collectorProbe(build)          # mode = suppression
+  applyAuthenticatedPairs(result)         # first write wins
+  if result.suppressedNothing:
+      provisional = false
+      existing convergence exit/throw applies
+      if result.passed: return
+  else:
+      provisional = true
+      fingerprint = candidateFingerprint(result)
+      if result.learnedNothing or not seen.add(fingerprint):
+          suppression = false             # next iteration is the clean probe
+          previousProbeFingerprint = undefined
+      continue
+
+on any loop exit:
+  if provisional:
+      suppression = false
+      previousProbeFingerprint = undefined
+      result = collectorProbe(build)      # one clean probe
+      if not result.passed: let the existing preflight report the failure
+      provisional = false
+```
+
+Rules:
+
+- The mark is cleared by a run that suppresses nothing. Any exit with it set forces a clean probe; a
+  clean run's failure is the reported failure, never "provisional".
+- Switching the VM mode invalidates the fixed-point skip. The probe fingerprint must include the mode,
+  or `previousProbeFingerprint` must be reset when suppression toggles
+  (`sc-derive-full-phase2-iteration-probe.ts:113`); otherwise an unchanged transaction is skipped and the
+  loop exits still provisional.
+- The clean exit belongs to the collector loop itself, not to `_deriveFullTransactionState`. The final
+  computed-output repair runs inside `stb.send()` through `_assertConvergedFinalTransaction`
+  (`StatefulTransactionBuilder.ts:7157`) and needs the same exit.
+- The provisional mark is **derivation-scoped**: a value marked in phase 1 stays marked into phase 2,
+  and only a clean run clears it. Do not scope the mark to one loop.
+- Refuse finalization while provisional as an **assertion** at `buildFinalized`
+  (`sc-build-stateful-function-call.ts`) and in the composed finalization. `.send()` does not pass through
+  `buildFinalized`: it goes `sendWithoutLogicalNonce` → `applyOverrides` → `stb.send()`, or
+  `composeDiscoveredGraph(...).send()`. The loop-exit clean probe is what protects those paths.
+- The composer reads participating outputs mid-derivation in its semantic claim loop
+  (`compose-semantic-claim-loop.ts:222`, imported by `compose.ts`). The composed finalization must refuse
+  while any participating builder is provisional. The claim loop may observe provisional values; it
+  cannot publish a stable claim set from them.
+- While suppression is on, the collector's progress and cycle set decides. Do not use the
+  `computeConvergenceKey` stable-key exit: it hashes what the probe revealed
+  (`sc-derive-full-phase2-convergence.ts:125`), and a suppressing run can produce a stable key while
+  values remain provisional. The existing exit and throw (`StatefulContract.ts:6252-6253`) apply to
+  runs that suppress nothing.
+- The cycle set is keyed by the applied candidate fingerprint. A repeated fingerprint, or a suppressing
+  run that learns nothing, turns suppression off and continues with the one-value loop from the current
+  state. No snapshot/restore is needed.
+- Authentication stays value-plus-ownership on every layer.
+- Speculative chunk growth stays off until step 5: `shouldGrowSpeculativeChunkRegion` receives
+  `outputVerificationMismatchObserved: false`
+  (`sc-derive-full-phase2-convergence.ts:109`), and the signal is reconnected there. Reconnecting it adds
+  outputs and changes the geometry, so step 5 lands it with byte identity and probe-count measurements.
+
+This answers the inferred removal reason: unconfirmed values cannot reach a final build or a finalized
+composition.
+
+### 7.5 Stateful integration
+
+- **Phase 2:** substitute the collector in `deriveFullPhase2IterationProbe`
+  (`sc-derive-full-phase2-iteration-probe.ts:66`). The collector run replaces the single
+  `evaluateProbeTrace`; a run that suppresses nothing is the faithful verdict. At standard limits this
+  waits for the recorder swap (§8.2). Keep the single-NFT/array exclusions.
+- **`firstWriteWins`:** keep it. It is live and independent of relaxation (`d673e3e39`), and 11 extractor
+  call sites pass `true` at this revision. The two phase-2 sites in
+  `sc-derive-full-phase2-apply-state-entries.ts` currently pass `false`; those are where the collector
+  reuses it.
+- **Phase 1 progressive entries:** the loop at `sc-derive-full-phase1-progressive-entries.ts:150`
+  currently feeds back one revealed commitment per pass. The collector reveals every authenticated
+  entry commitment the body verifies.
+- **Cleaned rescan:** `sc-derive-full-phase2-cleaned-rescan.ts:44` uses the same substitution.
+- **Final repair:** `repairComputedChunkOutput` (`stb-repair-computed-chunk-output.ts:110`) can fill
+  every authenticated failing output of the failing input in one sweep. The collector loop's clean exit
+  applies here too, inside `stb.send()`; the derivation-scoped mark carries into it.
+- **Runtime-loop transfers and deferred keccak:** keep the faithful trace. The removed implementation
+  did the same.
+
+### 7.6 Framed composer integration
+
+The composer uses the **same collector mechanism** in its own loop: suppress the failed checks it can
+trust, record them, and confirm with a clean run. This is step 4, and it is where the 61 s ladder titles
+spend their time.
+
+`readComputedDeclaration` becomes `readComputedDeclarations` (plural): one bounded walk that returns
+every close the run reached. `learnComputedValues` (`framed/composer/draft.ts:922`) then applies every
+authenticated match and repeats only for dependent values.
+
+A shared walk does **not** remove the 869 non-close reads. Only 190 of the 2,120 reads repeat a program
+(`draft.ts:931`), so the reads mostly hit different programs. A non-close read is how a
+`learnComputedValues` call stops (`draft.ts:936`); those reads shrink as calls and attempts shrink, not
+as walks are shared. The collector's gain here is that one attempt can learn every value a master's run
+reaches, which reduces attempts and therefore reads.
+
+#### Structural recipe cache
+
+The composer is where whole-program runs pile up: `take job` still does 1,014 evaluations and 274 walks
+per title, each over a composed transaction of about 670 KB in the no-proxy lane, and the last profile
+was VM-bound. A skipped read saves **either one full walk or one whole-program comparison**
+(`samePlainData`, `createVirtualMachine.ts:1232`). A whole-program copy (`copyOfPlainData`, `:1213`) is
+made only when an evaluation stops on a failed check, so the copy is saved only if that contract's
+evaluation is skipped too. The collector cannot remove these reads: it learns every value one walk
+reaches, but it cannot skip a walk that learns nothing.
+
+The recipe is **structural**, never a cache of values or program bytes. A memo keyed by program bytes was
+already measured and does not help, because only 190 of 2,120 reads repeat a program (`draft.ts:931`).
+
+- **Key:** artifact fingerprint, selector, argument shape, and plan/layout signature. Never cache values.
+- **Stored per contract run:** which placeholders the run resolves, which dependency layer each becomes
+  learnable in, and which transaction fields the run reads.
+- **Use:** walk a contract only in a layer where it can learn something, and only when a field it reads
+  has changed. The same rule gates the composer's **evaluations**: a contract whose read fields have not
+  changed produces the same evaluation result, so its evaluation is skipped too. Skip the final "nothing
+  left" walk after its last expected value.
+- **Fallback:** any deviation (an expected value not learned, or an unexpected one appearing) drops the
+  recipe for that key and runs the normal walk. Cap the cache with an LRU so memory stays bounded.
+- **Sizing comes first:** split the `tracedCloses` counter in `framed/composer/counters.ts` into three
+  outcomes (value learned, nothing to learn, run passed), and split `composerEvaluations` by caller. The
+  `evaluations` counter sits in the shared `machine.evaluate` wrapper
+  (`network/createVirtualMachine.ts:1205`), so it counts the composer and the final preflight. The mock
+  verifier uses libauth's `verify`, which closes over the inner evaluate
+  (`virtual-machine.js:146-150`) and bypasses that wrapper; its work appears in `phaseRuns`, not
+  `evaluations`. Add a `verify` counter to attribute it. Then run `take job` once with
+  `SOLIDC_DEBUG_FRAMED_COUNTS=1`. The nothing-learned reads, the repeated walks, and the repeated
+  evaluations are the most the recipe can save.
+
+The stateful path does not get a recipe: after step 3 a call costs one probe per dependency layer plus one
+clean run, so a recipe would save at most one cheap probe. The composer's per-walk cost is the reason the
+recipe lands here.
+
+`genesis.ts` and `proxy/deploy.ts` use the same reader.
+
