+## 2. Collector changes (`stateful/faithful-probe-trace.ts`)
+
+Commit 1 gave the collector VM a slot that holds only an operation wrapper. The pairing also needs to know, state by
+state, whether the collector recorded the state, so commit 2 widens the slot:
+
+- `BoundedProbeTraceCollector.observe(state)` (`:554`) returns `true` when it appends a projected observation and
+  `false` when it returns early: a state inside an inactive branch (commit 1), or an `OP_EQUALVERIFY` with no pending
+  observation (`:560`). The online pairing steps only on recorded states, so it sees exactly the sequence the
+  extractors later read.
+- New `export interface SuppressionHooks { beforeOperation?(state: unknown, recorded: boolean): void; readonly
+  operation: ProbeOperationWrapper; readonly firstFailure?: AuthenticationProgramStateCommon }`.
+  `evaluateSuppressedProbeTrace(provider, program, hooks: SuppressionHooks)` replaces the bare wrapper parameter; the
+  slot holds the hooks, and the dispatcher calls `slot.hooks?.operation`.
+- It returns `{ trace, faithfulTrace? } | undefined` instead of the trace. After each `slot.hooks.operation` call, the
+  first time `slot.hooks.firstFailure` is defined, the dispatcher stores `faithfulTrace =
+  collector.finish(slot.hooks.firstFailure)`. That is the trace a clean run of the same transaction returns, taken
+  before the run moves on: the projected observations through the suppressed check, the full stacks of the 96 before
+  it (`PROJECTED_PROBE_FULL_STACK_WINDOW`, `:389`), the evidence state and the failed terminal state. The run's own
+  trace cannot give the last three back later, because its window and evidence move on. `finish` may run mid-run: it
+  builds a new array and adds only the given state's stack to the evidence, as `observe` does for every recorded
+  state (`:554-616`); the run's own `finish` then also carries that state's evidence, which no reader of a suppressing
+  run's trace uses (spec/loops.md decision 8).
+- `installProbeVm` takes the slot. Its observer calls `slot?.hooks?.beforeOperation?.(state,
+  cached.collector.observe(state))` at both `observe` sites (`:707-716`, `:810`), and `(state, false)` on the
+  diagnostic early return for an opcode outside `PROBE_TRACE_OPCODES` (`:809`). A field such as
+  `lastObservationRecorded` would go stale there: `SOLIDC_DEBUG_DERIVE_TRACE_OPS` widens the observed set
+  (`:693-706`), and a stale `true` on an observed `OP_EQUAL` would clear commitment pins.
+- Commit 2 updates `test/unit/LiveLimitProbeVm.test.ts` test 7 to pass `{ operation }`.
+
+## 3. Collector probe (new `stateful/collector-probe.ts`)
+
+```ts
+export interface CollectorSession {
+  /** A non-minting NFT output of a category this builder settles (spec/loops.md section 3). */
+  ownsOutput(outputIndex: number): boolean;
+  /** A transfer or value output the builder planned (spec/loops.md section 3.3); absent = no value pins. */
+  ownsValueOutput?(outputIndex: number): boolean;
+  /** Default 4,096. At the bound suppression stops and later failures stand. */
+  readonly maxRecords?: number;
+}
+export interface CollectorRecord {
+  readonly kind: PinKind;
+  readonly outputIndex: number;
+  readonly expected: Uint8Array;
+  readonly actual: Uint8Array;
+  /** Recorded observations before this one: its index in the collector's trace. */
+  readonly ordinal: number;
+  /** For diagnostics only. */
+  readonly ip: number;
+}
+export interface CollectorProbeResult {
+  /** Same shape evaluateProbeTrace returns for the sparse observer, so every extractor works unchanged. */
+  readonly trace: AuthenticationProgramStateCommon[];
+  readonly records: readonly CollectorRecord[];
+  readonly suppressedCount: number;
+  /** suppressedCount === 0: this run is the faithful verdict. */
+  readonly suppressedNothing: boolean;
+  /** No state in the trace carries an error. With suppressedNothing, a clean pass. */
+  readonly passed: boolean;
+  /**
+   * Index in trace of the first suppressed check's observation, `records[0].ordinal`; -1 when nothing was
+   * suppressed. A clean run of the same transaction records `trace.slice(0, firstSuppressedTraceIndex + 1)` and
+   * then stops there.
+   */
+  readonly firstSuppressedTraceIndex: number;
+  readonly saturated: boolean;
+  /**
+   * The loops' cycle-set key: sha256 hex over the records' `${kind}:${outputIndex}:${hex(expected)}` lines, sorted
+   * and joined with `\n`; the empty string when there are no records.
+   */
+  readonly candidateFingerprint: string;
+  /**
+   * The trace a clean run of the same transaction returns, kept at the first suppression (section 2). Defined exactly
+   * when something was suppressed.
+   */
+  readonly faithfulTrace?: AuthenticationProgramStateCommon[];
+}
+/** Undefined while a full-debug override selects libauth debug(): the caller then probes cleanly. */
+export function evaluateCollectorProbe(
+  provider: NetworkProvider, program: ProbeProgram, session: CollectorSession,
+): CollectorProbeResult | undefined;
+
+export interface CollectorHooks extends SuppressionHooks {
+  /** `PROBE_TRACE_OPCODES` (seven opcodes, `faithful-probe-trace.ts:30-34`); merge them into the observer set. */
+  readonly opcodes: ReadonlySet<number>;
+  /** Step the pairing; `recorded` says whether the caller's own trace keeps this state. Call it for every state. */
+  beforeOperation(state: unknown, recorded: boolean): void;
+  /** Checks suppressed so far in this evaluation. */
+  readonly suppressedCount: number;
+  /** Set at the first suppression: the state a clean run ends with (the failed state, `ip` advanced). */
+  readonly firstFailure: AuthenticationProgramStateCommon | undefined;
+  result(): Omit<CollectorProbeResult, 'trace' | 'passed' | 'faithfulTrace'>;
+}
+/** For a VM the caller owns for one evaluation (deploy loop, final repair). Never attach to a cached shared VM. */
+export function createCollectorHooks(session: CollectorSession): CollectorHooks;
+```
+
+`evaluateCollectorProbe` creates hooks for the session, runs `evaluateSuppressedProbeTrace(provider, program, hooks)`
+(sparse only), and returns `undefined` when that does (the full-debug overrides). Otherwise it assembles the result
+from `hooks.result()`, the trace and the faithful trace; `passed` is the terminal state's error being undefined. The
+hooks count the `recorded` calls, so each record's `ordinal` is its observation's index in the trace: `finish` emits
+one entry per recorded observation, in order, before the evidence and terminal states (`:591`). Locating the record by
+`instructions` identity and `ip` would be wrong: libauth and the SDK share one decoded array per function body
+(`createVirtualMachine.ts:351`), and loops repeat an `ip`, so a passing check through the same outlined helper would
+match first.
+
+The deploy loop (its `observeValueChunkRuns` reads `stack[-6..-3]` at `OP_OUTPUTBYTECODE`, wider than the sparse
+projection, `projectState` `:519-552`) and the final repair (which observes live) use `createCollectorHooks` on their
+own observer VMs, passing their own recorded flag: the deploy, whether it pushed the state onto its trace; the repair,
+which keeps none, whether the state is in an active branch with an opcode in `opcodes`. Each calls `beforeOperation`
+for every state it observes, recorded or not, so a state's pairs never carry over. Recording an `OP_EQUALVERIFY` the
+collector would skip for having nothing pending is harmless: it pairs only with a pending introspection whose current
+value it names, as the extractors pair on a `debug()` trace. A caller that keeps a trace takes its own faithful trace
+when `firstFailure` appears (spec/loops.md section 8).
+
+Hook `beforeOperation(state, recorded)`: clear `pairs`; remember the previous recorded observation's opcode; when
+`recorded`, step the commitment pairing and the three value pairings with the state, keep what they return as this
+state's `pairs`, and count the observation. Pairs never carry over to a later state, so an unrecorded
+`OP_EQUALVERIFY` has none.
+
+Hook `operation(opcode, state, original)`:
+
+```text
+if opcode != OP_EQUALVERIFY: return original(state)
+if the previous recorded observation was OP_OUTPUTTOKENCATEGORY or OP_INPUTBYTECODE: return original(state)
+below, top = state.stack[-2], state.stack[-1]      # libauth byte arrays are immutable: no copies
+next = original(state)
+if next.error != AuthenticationErrorCommon.failedVerify: return next
+pair = this state's commitment pair if the transaction's value at its output (table in section 0) equals exactly
+       one of below/top; else the first of its bytecode, value and tokenAmount pairs that does; none: return next
+kind = pinKindOf(pair.opcode)                       # the online rule pairs only the four kinds' opcodes
+owned = kind == 'commitment' ? session.ownsOutput(i) : session.ownsValueOutput?.(i) === true
+if saturated or not owned: return next
+expected = the operand that is not the transaction's value
+record { kind, outputIndex: i, expected, actual, ordinal, ip }; suppressed += 1
+if records.length == 1:
+  firstFailure = { ...next, ip: next.ip + 1, stack: next.stack.slice(),
+    alternateStack: next.alternateStack.slice(), controlStack: next.controlStack.slice() }
+if records.length >= maxRecords: saturated = true
+return { ...next } without the error key            # applyError returns a shallow copy (errors.js:97)
+```
+
+`firstFailure` is the state a clean run ends with: libauth's step advances `ip` after an erroring operation
+(`virtual-machine.js:85-87`), and the redeem phase returns that state with its first error
+(`bch-2023-instruction-set.js:121-125`, `applyError` keeps an existing error, `errors.js:97-100`). The copies matter:
+later operations of the same run mutate the live stacks in place.
+
+Authentication uses the transaction's current value from the section 0 table; `pair.actual` is what the extractors
+compare against, and the two agree whenever the introspection pushed that value.
+
+The failed operation has already been charged; continuing changes no other metric.
+
+## 4. Tests
+
+Hand-built P2SH32 programs and providers as in `test/unit/LiveLimitProbeVm.test.ts` (`script`, `programWith`;
+`RegtestNetworkProvider` with and without `STANDARD_LIMITS`, `MockNetworkProvider` with them; no network calls).
+`test/unit/ProbePinPairing.test.ts` holds tests 4, 5, 10 and 14; `test/unit/CollectorProbe.test.ts` holds the rest
+(1-3, 6-9, 11-13, 15).
+
+1. K owned commitment pins against placeholders: `records.length === K`, `suppressedNothing === false`, and
+   `extractCommitmentMapFromTrace(trace, range, false, true)` returns the K expected values.
+2. A genuine `require` failure between pins: the run stops there; records hold only the earlier pins;
+   `firstSuppressedTraceIndex` points at the first of them. With a passing preserve pin through the same outlined
+   helper before the first suppressed check, the index still points at the suppressed one.
+3. Unowned output: not suppressed; the run stops.
+4. Outlined pin (`<idx> OTC <id> OP_INVOKE`, `OP_EQUALVERIFY` in the body) and the `OTC <expected> EQUALVERIFY`
+   operand order: both captured.
+5. A helper's own check while an outer OTC is pending (neither operand equals the actual): not suppressed.
+6. `OP_INPUTBYTECODE` then `OP_EQUALVERIFY`: never suppressed, through `evaluateCollectorProbe` and through
+   `createCollectorHooks` on a caller-owned VM.
+7. Saturation at `maxRecords: 2`: the third failure stands; `saturated === true`.
+8. Value pins: `value`, `bytecode` and `tokenAmount` captured with the right encodings only under
+   `ownsValueOutput`; `OP_OUTPUTTOKENCATEGORY` never, including `<i> OUTPUTTOKENAMOUNT <v> NUMEQUALVERIFY` followed by
+   a failing `<i> OUTPUTTOKENCATEGORY <cat> EQUALVERIFY` on a tokenless owned output.
+9. Faithful callers untouched: `evaluateProbeTrace` on the same program right after a collector run fails at the
+   first pin.
+10. Pairing equivalence: for the traces of tests 1-8, every pair a record names is one the extractors pair (drive
+    the pairing classes and both extractors over the same recorded sequence). The extractors return only final
+    values (`trace-commitment-helpers.ts:180-195`) and the value extractor drops indices
+    (`trace-verified-value-helpers.ts:304-309`), so use one pin per output and single-index ranges.
+11. Progress: `records[0].expected` equals what `evaluateProbeTrace` plus the extractor learns from a clean run of
+    the same transaction.
+12. Overrides: with `SC_FORCE_FULL_PROBE_TRACE=1`, `evaluateCollectorProbe` returns `undefined`.
+13. Reentry (SPEC C4): a session whose `ownsOutput` calls `evaluateCollectorProbe` on the same provider. The inner
+    call throws the nested-evaluation error and the outer call propagates it. Afterwards tests 1 and 9 pass on the
+    same provider: the slot is clear, and the next run resets the collector.
+14. Range: with a range that excludes a pending pin's output, `CommitmentPinPairing` drops the pin at an unrelated
+    `OP_EQUALVERIFY` exactly where the ranged extractor does, and the next in-range pin pairs the same in both.
+15. Faithful trace: for test 1's program, `faithfulTrace` equals what `evaluateProbeTrace` returns for the same program
+    state by state in `ip`, `instructions.length`, the opcode at `ip`, `stack` and `error`, the evidence state and the
+    terminal state included; a run that suppressed nothing has none. Instruction arrays are compared by content, not
+    identity: the evidence state's is built fresh by every `finish` (`faithful-probe-trace.ts:602-611`), libauth
+    decodes a redeem script again on every evaluation without the fast path (`bch-2023-instruction-set.js:105`), and
+    an invoked body is decoded per run (`bch-2026-functions.js:30`). Through `createCollectorHooks` on a caller-owned VM,
+    `suppressedCount` counts during the run, and `firstFailure` equals that clean run's terminal state in `ip`,
+    `stack` and `error`.
