+## 3. call.ts, genesis.ts, proxy/deploy.ts
+
+### 3.0 Speculation rule (call.ts, genesis.ts, proxy/deploy.ts)
+
+A loop that batches learns several values per attempt. This rule makes every decision it takes the base's.
+- A walk *speculates* when it applies a close other than its first; the first is the faithful reading (1.3). It
+  applies such a close only for a distinctive value: at least `DISTINCTIVE_BYTES` (20) bytes, and equal to no value the
+  loop already holds or still awaits. A shorter value is learned only as a walk's first close, as today.
+- A loop keeps its speculative values by slot: `speculative: Map<slot, Uint8Array>`. A close past a walk's first
+  sets its slot. A close of any kind (first, later, or the singular chain's) that would apply to a slot already in
+  `speculative` restores instead of applying: that value failed its close, so the batch was wrong, and state the loop
+  kept meanwhile may depend on it (call.ts's `calleeValues`, `perFrame`, `armsDecided`, `unscopedFrames`). Root views
+  can be re-learned this way, because the matcher also matches learned bytes (`call/call.ts:2732-2739`). Only a
+  restore and a confirming run empty the map.
+- A run *confirms* the speculative values only when it passes and it passed a close on each of them: a walk with
+  `notePassed` (1.1) whose passed values hold every value in `speculative`. `confirms(speculative, passed)` is this
+  test. A pass alone is not enough: a learned value can move the path, and a value the final path never compares
+  would reach the transaction where the base keeps a placeholder and refuses (`composer/draft.ts:400-407`).
+  Distinctive values make the bytes name their slot; equal bytes elsewhere do not block a confirmation (2.2).
+- The confirming runs are walks. call.ts's root walk already is one (3.1). Genesis's accepting read (3.2) and the hop
+  loops' runs (3.3b) are walks with `notePassed` while `origin` is set; a walk's `final` is what `evaluate` returns,
+  so the verdict is unchanged.
+- `origin` is the learned state at the start of the attempt whose walk first speculated, with that attempt's index,
+  taken once per loop. `pending` means some speculative value awaits confirmation. In call.ts it is
+  `speculative.size > 0`, and only call.ts confirms in the middle of a loop. In genesis and the hop loops a confirming
+  run ends the loop, so there `pending` is `origin !== undefined`, and the confirming exit clears `origin`. A run that
+  passes without confirming restores.
+- Restore: put `origin`'s state back, make the next attempt `origin.attempt` (`attempt = origin.attempt - 1;
+  continue;`), clear `origin` and `pending`, turn batching off for the rest of the loop, and bump
+  `speculationRestores`. From there the loop is the base's: same state, same index, same bound.
+- Restore:
+  0. on a pass that does not confirm (above);
+  1. before each terminal site of the loop while `pending`;
+  2. on any error leaving the attempt body while `pending`: one `try`/`catch` with no class filter, because signals
+     and refusals are decisions alike;
+  3. where the loop would leave on its bound with `origin` set, pending or not.
+
+  After the loop, assert `!pending`.
+- The last attempt a bound allows walks without `authenticate`, so every loop ends on a clean run (SPEC invariant 3).
+- Accounting: bounds stay today's (`call/call.ts:1847`, `deploy/genesis.ts:1305`, `proxy/deploy.ts:1987`, and `:2495`
+  under the cap `:293`), with no `+1` and no extension. A restore rewinds instead: the discarded attempts do not count
+  against the bound (the counters still count them). One restore per loop keeps a loop under twice its bound.
+  - The stateful loops' rule (SPEC L1: every run counts, no rewind) cannot hold here. A restore drops every value
+    learned since `origin`, first closes included, because a wrong provisional value cannot be re-learned: most kinds
+    match only their sentinel or placeholder (`call/call.ts:2722-2796`, `proxy/deploy.ts:2101-2131`, `:2566-2578`),
+    and `perFrame`, `armsDecided` and `unscopedFrames` are sticky.
+  - The bounds are tight (`settleHop`: one attempt per value plus one, `:2492-2501`). A replay left with `bound - u`
+    attempts where the base had `bound - origin.attempt` would turn a base success into an exhaustion refusal
+    (`call/call.ts:2883-2924`, `deploy/genesis.ts:1335`, `proxy/deploy.ts:2695-2705`).
+  - The hop cap stays an index bound: it only bounds a plan that never hands anything back (`proxy/deploy.ts:286-291`).
