+The rest of the chain (`:2710-2877`) reads `reading` unchanged.
+
+**`rootLearningOf(close)` and `applyRootLearnings(closes, candidate)`.** Both are new closures extracted from the
+chain, in the chain's order, so a close that two kinds could match resolves as today. `rootLearningOf` first takes
+`closeReading(close, maximumCommitmentBytes)`, the bound the singular chain applies: an `ok` reading tries the
+non-empty kinds (`:2721`); `implausible-length` with a close tries only the empty NFT commitment (`:2710`); anything
+else matches nothing. The kinds:
+- the empty NFT commitment (`:2710-2720`, a close with an empty computed value);
+- the retval in both orders (`:2722-2729`);
+- the views, still declared, with first-still-declared semantics (`:2732-2739`);
+- the events (`:2742-2746`);
+- the send locks (`:2747-2752`);
+- the token sends (`:2754-2764`);
+- the call values (`:2766-2774`, including the `sharesPayee` reset of `calleeValues`);
+- the send amounts (`:2791-2796`).
+
+Typed-call values (`:2776-2789`) and the payee branch skip (`:2800-2808`) stay in the chain and are never
+authenticated (F5).
+
+`applyRootLearnings` applies the closes in run order. It matches each close against the state as already updated
+in this walk (so "the first one still declared" moves on as consecutive reads would), but skips a slot already written
+in the same walk. Slots are `retval`, `view:i`, `event:i`, `lock:i`, `amount:i`, `token:run:i` and `call:i`. It
+returns `{ applied, later }`: the count it applied and the `[slot, value]` pairs it applied from later closes.
+`rootLearningOf(close)` returns the learning with its `slot`. A later close applies only a distinctive value (section
+3.0: at least 20 bytes, equal to no other root value, learned or sentinel); any other later close is skipped and
+learned by a later walk. The single-read chain's application is a first close; one whose slot is in `speculative`
+restores instead (section 3.0).
+The single-read chain then calls `rootLearningOf(reading)` + `applyRootLearnings([reading])` for those kinds, so
+there is one implementation.
