+## 9. Equivalence analysis
+
+**Recorder with the hook vs `debug()` (live / non-fast).** `debug(program)` =
+`instructionSet.evaluate(program, { stateEvaluate: proxyDebug })`. `proxyDebug` records
+`[initial, after op1 .. after opN]` per phase, then `finalResult` (`virtual-machine.js` `stateDebug`/`debug`). The
+recorder runs the same `instructionSet.evaluate` with `stateEvaluate` = stock `untilComplete(stateClone(s),
+stateStepMutate)` + exit hook, and snapshots before each op. Per phase it records `[clone(initial), after op1 ..
+after op(N-1)]`, then the exit (= after opN, post-`continue`), then the final snapshot. A phase with no ops records
+`[initial]` against `[exit = clone(initial)]`. A refused phase (malformed redeem, non-push unlocking, bytecode
+length) records nothing in either. `snapshotProgramState` copies like `partiallyCloneProgramState` except
+`functionTable`. Equal state for state, `functionTable` aside.
+
+**`installCachedP2shEvaluator` at standard limits, why not used.** It enforces only bytecode length itself
+(parameter = `limits.maximumInputBytes`, which equals `ConsensusBch2026.maximumBytecodeLength` 10,000); every other
+limit stays in the instruction set. Its checks and messages match libauth 2023 `evaluate`
+(`bch-2023-instruction-set.js:31-140`) in order. Its differences:
+(a) unlocking pushes are views (`decodeAuthenticationInstructionViews`, `:21`) and redeem stack items are shared
+    instead of `structuredClone`d (`:272-275`) - content-equal;
+(b) push-only by opcode <= OP_16 after the malformed check (`:200`), equivalent to `isPushOnly`;
+(c) a per-provider decoded-redeem cache keyed by the authenticated P2SH lock (`decodeCachedP2sh` `:132`, 64-entry
+    FIFO), which shares instruction arrays across evaluations;
+(d) `bumpFramedCounter('phaseRuns')` (`:167`), which would skew framed counters on live;
+(e) `onPhaseComplete(p2shResult)` at `:293` even for a malformed redeem script, where `debug()` records no phase, so
+    the fast-path recorder already diverges there (open question 5).
+For a standard-limit input it would be equivalent except (c), (d) and (e). The hook has none of them, and at
+<= 10 KB the decode and clone it skips cost microseconds.
+
+**Sparse vs reduced `debug()`: not state-for-state, by design.** The reduced debug trace also contains: inactive-
+branch observations and their effect on the awaiting flag; identity-count `OP_2DROP` states (`reduceProbeTrace`
+`:706` admits them, `PROBE_TRACE_OPCODES` excludes `OP_2DROP`); and a phantom observation when a failed step's
+next instruction is a probe opcode (`ip` advances on error). The sparse trace projects stacks to the top 2-4 items
+outside the last 96 observations, adds an evidence pseudo-state (`ip: -1`) and projects the terminal. Exact equality
+is therefore checked against the replay (Change G). Old vs new is checked at extractor and byte level (section 12).
+
+## 10. What the observer path uses from the fast block (`:923-1099`), and what a live provider gets
