+## 0. Decisions made here (beyond the brief)
+
+| # | decision | why |
+|---|---|---|
+| D1 | The log gate is an artifact-level union over every contract input: `debug.logs`, every `functionDebug[*].logs`, every `invokeDebug[*].logs`. It does not narrow to the called selector. | The printer resolves its artifact by contract-name prefix (`debugging.ts:123`) and its entry selector by function name (`debugging.ts:187-196`), so an exact per-selector mirror would copy those quirks. A union is a superset of what the printer can print, so output is never lost. The decided wording is also artifact-level ("the covenant artifact's debug info has log entries"). Cost of the superset: an artifact that logs only in function A still pays the debug when B is called. |
+| D2 | The gate and the density check are `TransactionBuilder` methods (`hasDebugLogs()`, `assertOperationCostDensity()`), shared by both send paths. | One implementation means one error type and one message on both paths. Prototype methods can be spied in tests (`vi.spyOn(TransactionBuilder.prototype, ...)`), which test (iii) needs to force the old behaviour; an ESM free function cannot be spied. |
+| D3 | The density check uses the cached observerless VM `createVirtualMachineBch2026ForProvider(provider, undefined, { standard })`, priced as the deciding VM (SPEC S1): the provider's policy on the plain path, `standard: true` on the stateful path. | The factory is the one the debugger used (`debugging.ts:60-75`), `_preflightRecursiveBuilder` (`stateful/StatefulContract.ts:14463`) and `covenantObjectionOf` (`message-bus/preflight.ts:979-990`). On the stateful path it reproduces the old probe's verdicts exactly; on the plain path it matches the provider's verifier. |
+| D4 | The density refusal is recognised with the old probe's own regex, `/exceed the operation cost density limit/`, on `state.error`. | libauth (`bch-2025-instruction-set.js:41`) and the SDK fast path (`network/createVirtualMachine.ts:450-454`) both raise `AuthenticationErrorBch2025.excessiveOperationCost`, "Program attempted an operation that would exceed the operation cost density limit."; the regex also covers libauth's OP_POW variant, like the old probe did. |
+| D5 | `FailedTransactionEvaluationError` is built with `failingInstructionPointer = -1`, so it carries no source line. `libauthErrorMessage` is `` `${state.error}\n${GUIDANCE}` ``. | `evaluate()` keeps no trace, so there is no caller step to attribute an OP_INVOKE frame to. `getLocationDataForInstructionPointer` returns line 0 for a negative pointer (`Errors.ts:214-217`), which yields the line-less message form. A newline keeps the libauth sentence as the first line, which is what `bench/solidity-corpus/semantic-runner.mjs` `vmReason` reads (`split('\n')[0]`). |
+| D6 | The stateful density check runs only where the old debug ran (`!relaxedConsensus`). The plain-path check runs at every limit, like the swallowed debug it replaces. | The relaxed lane stays byte-for-byte what it is today, which is the evidence the design relies on ("every mocknet test exercises the post-change path"). Open question Q5. |
+| D7 | `message-bus/preflight.ts` keeps its `builder.build()` call; only the duplicate `hexToBin` is removed. | `test/e2e/L4BuildRefusalScope.test.ts:166-170` pins the literal text `builder.build()` in `sendPreflightedMessageBusTransaction`. Option B (switch and edit the pin) is in section 5. |
+| D8 | The fixed-point set in the progressive-entries loop stores `binToHex(sha256(encodeTransaction(tx)))`. | It keeps exactly today's equality relation (encoding equality), with no identity cache. `sha256 as sha256Sync` is already imported there. `fingerprintLibauthTransaction` would also save the encode, but it hashes by `Uint8Array` identity cache, which is a new semantic for this loop. The saving is small next to the `evaluateProbeTrace` each pass runs. |
